import{at as e,n as t,rt as n,xt as r}from"./chunks/framework.DIOWbp6D.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"The paradigm","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"paradigm/index.md","filePath":"paradigm/index.md"}`),a={name:`paradigm/index.md`};function o(t,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),n(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="the-paradigm" tabindex="-1">The paradigm <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-paradigm" aria-label="Permalink to “The paradigm”">​</a></h1><p>Handl is a categorical language: computation is described as <strong>arrows</strong>, not applied as functions.</p><p>In a functional language, you write <code>f(x)</code>: a function is applied to a value, produces a value, and whatever effects it has are hidden inside the closure. In Handl, you compose arrows: <code>f &gt;&gt;&gt; g</code> feeds the output of one computation into the next, <code>f &amp;&amp;&amp; g</code> runs two computations side by side, <code>f ||| g</code> chooses between them, and <code>loop</code> feeds output back as input. The result is a structure you can inspect, not a black box you can only call.</p><h2 id="the-closed-arrow-calculus" tabindex="-1">The closed arrow calculus <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-closed-arrow-calculus" aria-label="Permalink to “The closed arrow calculus”">​</a></h2><p>The kernel&#39;s computation model is a closed arrow calculus in the Lindley-Wadler-Yallop tradition, extended with graded effects and higher-categorical structure. Six combinators are enough:</p><ul><li><code>arr</code>: lift a pure value into an arrow</li><li><code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>: sequential composition</li><li><code>first</code>: apply an arrow to the first component of a pair</li><li><code>choice</code>: alternative composition (the <code>|||</code> surface form)</li><li><code>loop</code>: feedback</li><li><code>app</code>: application</li></ul><p>No lambda, no variables-as-functions, no sugar hiding the structure. What you see is what executes.</p><h2 id="everything-is-data" tabindex="-1">Everything is data <a class="header-anchor" href="#everything-is-data" aria-label="Permalink to “Everything is data”">​</a></h2><p>The deeper bet is representation. Programs are 1-morphisms; laws and rewrites are 2-morphisms; higher transformations extend the structure into higher dimensions. Composition is dimension-indexed, with explicit boundaries, rewriting rules, and coherence conditions, so higher-dimensional structures compose consistently.</p><p>Because arrows are data, computation becomes:</p><ul><li><strong>inspectable</strong>: you can read, diff, and search a program&#39;s structure</li><li><strong>transformable</strong>: rewrites and optimizations are themselves arrows</li><li><strong>serializable</strong>: an arrow survives a round trip through JSON, YAML, or any engine&#39;s memory model</li><li><strong>interpretable</strong>: tree-walk, bytecode, Cranelift, LLVM, Triton, and CUDA backends all consume the same representation</li></ul><h2 id="language-as-data" tabindex="-1">Language as data <a class="header-anchor" href="#language-as-data" aria-label="Permalink to “Language as data”">​</a></h2><p>A language in Handl is not hardcoded into an engine. It is a declarative <code>Language</code> value: syntax, grammar, AST shapes, evaluation schemes, type rules, and transformations, described as data. Engines are generic interpreters of that data, which is what makes new languages and new backends cheap to add.</p><h2 id="graded-effects" tabindex="-1">Graded effects <a class="header-anchor" href="#graded-effects" aria-label="Permalink to “Graded effects”">​</a></h2><p>Effects are first-class but disciplined. The kernel implements the Graded Modal Sanada Arrow Calculus: operations carry grades, handlers interpret them, and the grade system tracks what a computation is allowed to do. Effects are algebraic (Sanada 2024) and handlers are where meaning is supplied. Grades generalize further into <strong>modes</strong>: an MTT-style axis where grades, coeffects, and effect rows are all mode-algebra instances over ordinary data.</p><h2 id="why-it-matters" tabindex="-1">Why it matters <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-it-matters" aria-label="Permalink to “Why it matters”">​</a></h2><p>Most language infrastructure forces a trade-off: expressiveness lives in the syntax, but the semantics is locked inside a runtime you cannot inspect. Handl removes the trade-off. The mathematics (arrows, effects, handlers, higher categories) is the substrate; tooling, libraries, and products are layers on top.</p><p><strong>Architectural principle:</strong> the Kernel defines a compositional, higher-categorical computational model. Engines interpret and transform its representations. Libraries provide runtime-agnostic abstractions, while Kits assemble them into usable environments. Tools bridge Handl with external systems, and Hosts expose the ecosystem to users and development environments.</p><p>For the full type system that sits on this substrate (dependent types, cubical equality, effect signatures, and modes), see the <a href="/docs/learn/">type system overview</a>.</p><p>The <a href="/docs/story/">story</a> covers where this comes from; the <a href="/docs/ecosystem/bibliography.html">bibliography</a> lists the papers; the <a href="/docs/ecosystem/">ecosystem</a> shows how the repos fit together.</p>`,20)]])}var s=t(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};